#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:4
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &kturtle;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: using-kturtle.docbook:7
#, no-c-format
msgid "Here is a screenshot of &kturtle; in action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: using-kturtle.docbook:13
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kturtle; Main Window"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The main window of &kturtle; has two main parts: <link linkend=\"the-code-"
"editor\">the code editor</link> (3) on the left where you type the &logo; "
"commands, and <link linkend=\"the-canvas\">the canvas</link> (4) on the "
"right where the instructions are visualized. <link linkend=\"the-canvas"
"\">The canvas</link> is the turtle's playground: it is on the canvas that "
"the turtle actually moves and draws. The three other places on the main "
"window are: <link linkend=\"the-menubar\">the menu bar</link> (1) from where "
"all the actions can be reached, <link linkend=\"the-toolbar\">the toolbar</"
"link> (2) that allows you to quickly select the most used actions, and <link "
"linkend=\"the-statusbar\">the statusbar</link> (5) where you will find "
"feedback on the state of &kturtle;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:21
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Code Editor"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:22
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the code editor you type the &logo; commands. It has all of the features "
"you would expect from a modern editor. Most of its features are found in the "
"<link linkend=\"the-edit-menu\">Edit</link> and the <link linkend=\"the-"
"tools-menu\">Tools</link> menus. The code editor can be docked on each "
"border of the main window or it can be detached and placed anywhere on your "
"desktop."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:23
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You have several ways to get some code in the editor. The easiest way is to "
"use an already-made example: choose <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Open Examples</guimenuitem> </menuchoice> in the <link "
"linkend=\"the-file-menu\">File menu</link> and click on a file. The filename "
"will tell you what the example is about (&eg; <filename>square.logo</"
"filename> will draw a square). The file you choose will be opened in the "
"<link linkend=\"the-code-editor\">the code editor</link>, you can then use "
"<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Execute Commands</"
"guimenuitem> </menuchoice> to run the code if you like."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:27
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can open &logo; files by choosing <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Open...</guimenuitem> </menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:29
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The third way is to directly type your own code in the editor or to copy/"
"paste some code from this user guide."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:31
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The cursor position is indicated in <link linkend=\"the-statusbar\">the "
"statusbar</link>, on the right with the Line number and Column number."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:37
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Canvas"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:38
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The canvas is the area where the commands are visualized, where the commands "
"<quote>draw</quote> a picture. In other words, it is the turtle's "
"playground. After getting some code in the <link linkend=\"the-code-editor"
"\">the code editor</link>, and executing it using <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Execute Commands</guimenuitem> </menuchoice>, two "
"things can happen: either the code executes fine, and will you most likely "
"see something change on the canvas; or you have made an error in your code "
"and there will be a message telling you what error you made."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid "This message should help you to resolve the error."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:42
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The picture that is drawn can be saved as an image (using "
"<menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Save Canvas</guimenuitem> </"
"menuchoice>) or printed (using <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Print...</guimenuitem> </menuchoice>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:48
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Menubar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:49
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the menubar you find all the actions of &kturtle;. They are in the "
"following groups: <guimenu>File</guimenu>, <guimenu>Edit</guimenu>, "
"<guimenu>View</guimenu>, <guimenu>Tools</guimenu>, <guimenu>Settings</"
"guimenu>, and <guimenu>Help</guimenu>. This section describes them all."
msgstr ""

